  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>There is no built-in way to know whether a text-to-speech engine actually said what it was given — commercial audiobook and audio-Bible producers catch misreads only with human proof-listeners. The industry-standard automated substitute is <strong>ASR back-transcription with dual-engine consensus</strong>:</p>
  <ol class="av-steps">
    <li><strong>Transcribe</strong> every chapter's audio back to text with a speech-recognition engine (OpenAI <em>Whisper</em>).</li>
    <li><strong>Normalize</strong> both the transcript and the exact text we sent ElevenLabs identically (numbers, punctuation, casing, parentheses), so formatting differences don't masquerade as errors.</li>
    <li><strong>Align &amp; diff</strong> the two, classifying every difference as a <em>dropped</em>, <em>added</em>, or <em>substituted</em> word.</li>
    <li><strong>Confirm with a second, independent engine</strong> (Kaldi/<em>Vosk</em>) and keep a flag <em>only where both engines disagree with the text at the same spot</em>. This suppresses the errors of any single recognizer — the core reliability trick.</li>
    <li><strong>Tier &amp; triage</strong> what survives: the audio is faithful, but both engines share one bias — they mishear rare biblical names, foreign words, units, and archaic vocabulary the same way. Tiering separates genuinely meaning-changing signals from that shared noise.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>Both engines were run over all 1,189 chapters. 1,054 chapters raised at least one raw flag; after classification:</p>
  <div class="av-tierbar">
    <div class="av-tierbox t1"><div class="n">22</div><div class="l">Tier 1 — Priority review</div><div class="d">A dropped negation, a 3+ word phrase, or a real hallucination. Every one was manually verified — see below.</div></div>
    <div class="av-tierbox t2"><div class="n">275</div><div class="l">Tier 2 — Rare vocabulary</div><div class="d">Isolated uncommon words both engines garbled (ephod, jacinth, manna, plumb, censers). Not defects.</div></div>
    <div class="av-tierbox t3"><div class="n">546</div><div class="l">Name / genealogy noise</div><div class="d">Runs of rare Hebrew names both engines mishear identically (1 Chronicles genealogies, place lists).</div></div>
  </div>

