  <p class="av-note"><strong>Bottom line:</strong> not a single Tier-1 item is a real audio defect. The dual-engine method is sensitive enough to catch a dropped “no” or “not” — and here every such flag resolved to a word that <em>is</em> spoken, just mis-aligned by the recognizers on a repeated or boundary word.</p>

  <h2>Method notes &amp; caveats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Engines:</strong> OpenAI Whisper (small.en) + Kaldi/Vosk (en-us 0.22) — different model families, so their mistakes don't correlate. A flag needs both to agree.</li>
    <li><strong>What it can miss:</strong> a defect that both engines happen to mis-transcribe in the same way could slip through. In practice the strong signals (dropped negations, fabricated sentences) are exactly what recognizers get right, so confidence is high. A random human spot-check remains the belt-and-suspenders backstop.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared blind spot:</strong> both engines fail on rare Hebrew names, foreign words (Daniel 5's “mene mene … parsin”), liturgical terms (“Selah”), units (seah, ephah, cor), and archaic vocabulary — which is why 821 of the 1,054 raw flags are that noise, not defects.</li>
    <li><strong>Reusable:</strong> the same pipeline can re-validate any newly generated or regenerated book.</li>
  </ul>

